{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Research: mutation testing in active OSS CI (2026-08-10)\n\nOperator direction is supported, with one terminology correction: mutmut mutates production code, not new/modified test files. Changed tests should trigger the narrow production shard they cover, and that shard should run using the relevant tests.\n\nObserved maintained-project patterns:\n- PostHog/posthog-python runs a targeted mutation job on PRs/main only when one named production file or either of its paired test files changed. It uses one Python version, a normal pytest timeout, max-children=1, and a cache key hashing the source and tests; an exact cache hit skips the run. New survivors hard-fail. Source: https://github.com/PostHog/posthog-python/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml\n- 567-labs/instructor keeps mutation manual and targets one async-retry function family. It caps the job at 30 minutes, max-children=4, pins mutmut==3.6.0, runs from a clean git archive, and enforces explicit budgets for known structural survivors and one known infinite-loop timeout rather than requiring a misleading perfect score. Source: https://github.com/567-labs/instructor/blob/main/.github/workflows/mutation.yml\n- Trail of Bits trailmark runs its small full mutation suite on PR/main, cancels superseded runs, and always prints results. Source: https://github.com/trailofbits/trailmark/blob/main/.github/workflows/mutation.yml\n- GeoParquet runs the broad suite nightly/manual with a hard job timeout. It retries only transient DuckDB-extension installation, treats mutmut execution failure as failure, treats zero generated/parsed mutants as a hard failure, compares against a checked-in score floor, and always uploads artifacts. Source: https://github.com/geoparquet/geoparquet-io/blob/main/.github/workflows/nightly.yml\n\nOfficial mutmut guidance confirms two cache hazards and mitigations: result-affecting config changes invalidate cache automatically; non-Python dependency/config changes need watched files and on_dependency_change=rerun to avoid stale results. Its per-mutant timeout is derived from baseline test duration plus a constant times a multiplier. Source: https://mutmut.readthedocs.io/en/latest/#detecting-dependency-and-config-changes and #unstable-configs\n\nRecommended rebar design: in Gerrit Validate, detect newly added/modified tests, map them to an existing selected behavioral-core shard (or the directly covered source functions), run the normal shard once as a baseline, then run only those mutants with a pinned Python/mutmut version, low bounded concurrency, and per-mutant plus whole-job timeouts. Cache only on an exact hash of production source + selected tests + lock/config inputs. A zero-mutant run, baseline-test failure, tool crash, timeout-budget overrun, new survivor, or score regression must be distinguishable in output. Do not blanket-retry mutation outcomes; retry only independently identified infrastructure setup failures. Keep the broader baseline sweep scheduled/manual."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786395049860512001,"uuid":"a3db84a2-0be7-4c00-a6c7-fc8563b3d97b"}